Which top-level funcs a Model-receiver target emits free, and which it does
not.

`viaBump` is the transitive half. It touches no var of its own and reaches
`log` only through the call, so by its own body it is free -- and a free
`ViaBump` calling the Model method `bump` would spell `m.bump(…)`, one
function further out.

`countdown` is the control: a top-level func that touches nothing is still free,
under its exported name, in all three. It recurses because passInlinePure
substitutes a pure one away otherwise, and a golden with no free function in
it would leave the two denies below asserting nothing.

deny * `(?m)^func Bump\(` -- a func that writes a package var is a Model method; free, its body names an undefined m
deny * `(?m)^func ViaBump\(` -- and so is every top-level caller of one, or the call site names that m instead
-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
}

var log string = ""
var n int = 4

func bump(k int) int {
    log = log + "!"
    return k * 2
}

func viaBump(a int) => bump(a) + 1

func countdown(a int) int {
    if a <= 0 {
        return 0
    }
    return countdown(a - 1) + 1
}

component main node {
    text(value="{viaBump(n)}{countdown(n)}{log}")
}

window {
    main
}
